WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.200
Querying is where a hunt hypothesis becomes an evidence search.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.260
The exact syntax depends on the SIEM, data lake, EDR console, or log platform,

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.515
but the thinking is provider-neutral.

00:00:09.515 --> 00:00:13.268
Start with the behavior, identify the required fields, select the data source,

00:00:13.268 --> 00:00:14.560
define the time window,

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.460
and write a query or filter that returns enough context to reason about the event.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.845
A good query is not just a string that produces results. It is a documented test of a hunt

00:00:22.845 --> 00:00:23.270
idea.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:26.510
Pivots help the analyst move from one clue to related evidence.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:30.480
A host-to-user pivot asks which identities were active on a system around a time of

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.090
interest.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:35.260
A user-to-source-IP pivot asks where an account authenticated from and whether that

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.285
location is normal.

00:00:36.285 --> 00:00:40.095
A process-to-parent-process pivot asks what launched a suspicious command. A

00:00:40.095 --> 00:00:43.920
domain-to-DNS-clients pivot asks which systems resolved a destination.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.340
A hash-to-endpoints pivot asks where a file appeared. An alert-to-timeline pivot asks what

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.010
happened before and after a signal.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:54.190
Each pivot should have a purpose tied to the hypothesis. Noise reduction is necessary,

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.315
but it can be dangerous.

00:00:55.315 --> 00:00:58.890
Large environments produce routine administrative tools, software updates,

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.915
cloud automation, service accounts, vulnerability scanners,

00:01:01.915 --> 00:01:04.360
and user behavior that may look odd in isolation.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.460
Filters help remove known benign patterns, but over-filtering can hide the signal.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.425
If you exclude all administrative accounts,

00:01:10.425 --> 00:01:12.810
you may miss compromised administrator activity.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.235
If you exclude all internal tools, you may miss misuse of those tools.

00:01:16.235 --> 00:01:20.620
A better approach is to filter deliberately and document why each exclusion is reasonable.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.650
Baselines are useful for reducing noise.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.720
Instead of asking whether a process ever runs, ask whether it runs on this host type,

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.280
under this parent, by this user, during this time window.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.860
Instead of asking whether a domain is rare globally,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.050
ask whether it is rare for this business unit or endpoint group.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:36.505
Baselines are not perfect proof,

00:01:36.505 --> 00:01:40.090
but they help separate ordinary from unusual. Save queries and assumptions.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.320
A hunt should be repeatable by another analyst.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.550
Save the query text, data source, time window, filters, field names, known exclusions,

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:47.735
and any limitations.

00:01:47.735 --> 00:01:49.620
If a field name changes or a parser breaks,

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.360
future reviewers need to know what the query depended on.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:56.720
The beginner mistake is to treat querying as the hunt itself. Querying is a tool.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.860
The hunt is the structured process of asking a question, collecting evidence,

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.270
pivoting carefully, reducing noise responsibly,

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.185
and documenting what the evidence supports.

00:02:04.185 --> 00:02:07.480
Provider-neutral thinking helps when teams use more than one tool.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:11.045
One SIEM may call a field process_name, another may call it image,

00:02:11.045 --> 00:02:14.240
and an EDR console may show the same concept in a different panel.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.950
The hunter should understand the concept behind the field. That understanding makes the

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.760
hunt portable and reduces dependence on memorized syntax.
